#{[17592186567078 "What is the solution to the following system of inequalities?\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y-5\\le 2 \\\\ 2y+7>11\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution is\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$y\\le $\n{{field-3}}."] [17592186573272 "What is the solution to the following system of inequalities?\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}p-2>3 \\\\ 1-2p\\le -7\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186580227 "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1-{x}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ \\frac{8}{9{x}^{3}}, & 1<x\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{3}].$"] [17592186580228 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}(4-x),\\, & 2\\le x\\le 5 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<2 \\\\ h(x),\\, & 2\\le x\\le 5 \\\\ 1,\\, & x>5.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x).$"] [17592186580239 "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & 0<x<1, \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}, & 1\\le x<3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$"] [17592186580240 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-2x},\\, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0,\\, & x<0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<0 \\\\ h(x),\\, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x).$"] [17592186580248 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{2-2x},\\, & x\\ge 1 \\\\ 0,\\, & x<1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<1 \\\\ h(x),\\, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $h(x)=${{field-1}}."] [17592186580268 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{1-x},\\, & x\\ge 1 \\\\ 0,\\, & x<1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<1 \\\\ h(x),\\, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x).$"] [17592186580279 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<0, \\\\ {x}^{2},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 1,\\, & x>1,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nfind $P(\\frac{1}{2}<X\\le 2).$"] [17592186580294 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{e}^{-|x/4|}}{8},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}h(x),\\, & x<0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{{e}^{-x/4}}{2},\\, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $h(x)=${{field-1}}."] [17592186580305 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2}, & x<0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2}, & x\\ge 0,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nfind $P(X\\ge 4).$"] [17592186580306 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{1-x/3},\\, & x\\ge 3 \\\\ 0,\\, & x<3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<3 \\\\ h(x),\\, & x\\ge 3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $h(x)=${{field-1}}."] [17592186580320 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ \\frac{x}{2}, & 0\\le x<2 \\\\ 1, & x\\ge 2,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nfind $P(1\\le X\\le 3).$"] [17592186580322 "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}, & 0\\le x<1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\,x, & 1\\le x\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{4}].$"] [17592186580880 "Given that the random variable $X∼{χ}^{2}(8),$ calculate $P(X<3).$ Round your final answer to four decimal places.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x≈6.30168\n$$\n\n*The probability density function ${χ}^{2}(k)$ is given by*\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{2}^{k/2}Γ\\left(\\frac{k}{2}\\right)}{x}^{k/2-1}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186580909 "Given that $X∼{χ}^{2}(8),$ what is $P(X<3)$ expressed as an integral?\n\n*Hint: The probability density function ${χ}^{2}(k)$ is given by*\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{2}^{k/2}Γ\\left(\\frac{k}{2}\\right)}{x}^{k/2-1}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186580958 "Given that the random variable $X∼{χ}^{2}(4),$ calculate $P(X<9).$ Round your final answer to four decimal places.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{9}x{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x≈3.75560\n$$\n\n*The probability density function ${χ}^{2}(k)$ is given by*\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{2}^{k/2}Γ\\left(\\frac{k}{2}\\right)}{x}^{k/2-1}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186581655 "Given that $X$ is a continuous random variable with the following probability density function, compute $\\text{Var}[X].$ \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{8}-\\frac{1}{4},\\, & 2\\le x\\le 6, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186581681 "The continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x)$ stated below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{5}{4},$ compute $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{{x}^{6}},\\, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186581697 "The continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x)$ stated below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=1,$ compute $\\text{Var}[X].$ \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1-x, & 0<x<1 \\\\ \\frac{10(x-1)}{{x}^{6}},\\, & x\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186581713 "The continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x)$ stated below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{11}{6},$ compute $\\text{Var}[X].$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{27}, & 0<x<3, \\\\ \\frac{9}{{x}^{4}}, & 3\\le x, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186581719 "The continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x)$ stated below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]≈0.6931,$ compute $\\text{Var}[X].$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+4}{24}, & -4<x<2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}, & 2\\le x<4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186581738 "The continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x)$ stated below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=2,$ compute $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12(x-1)}{{x}^{5}},\\, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186582364 "Find the limit at $x=1$ of the piecewise function $f(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-1\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<1 \\\\ 2\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x=1 \\\\ 2-{x}^{2}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186582368 "Find the right-sided limit at $x=32$ for the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le 32 \\\\ \\sqrt[5]{x}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>32.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {32}^{+}}f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186582373 "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}+1 & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{x}+2 & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186582387 "For the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x\\pi }{4}\\right)+3x\\, & \\text{ for }\\,-2<x\\le 1 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{x\\pi }{4}\\right)+kx\\, & \\text{ for }\\,1<x<4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nit is known that $\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)$ exists. What is the value of constant $k?$"] [17592186582390 "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed between the curve $y=|x|-1$ and the line $y=2.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}xy{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}xy{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-1,2]$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}xy{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-1,3]$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}xy{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [0,3]$"] [17592186582391 "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=-x+2.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+xy, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+xy)\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\nI. $R=[-2,1]\\times [-1,2]$\n\nII. $R=[-3,2]\\times [-1,4]$\n\nIII. $R=[-3,1]\\times [2,4]$"] [17592186582393 "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{2}-1$ and $y=x+1.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y+y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y+y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\nI. $R=[-1,2]\\times [-1,3]$\n\nII. $R=[-1,1]\\times [-1,1]$\n\nIII. $R=[-1,3]\\times [-1,2]$"] [17592186582394 "Find the right-sided limit at $x=-4$ of the piecewise function $f(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-16\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le -4 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>-4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186582396 "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed between the curves $y={x}^{2}-4$ and $y=-x+2.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{3}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-3,2]\\times [-4,5]$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-5,5]$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}({x}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-4,4]\\times [-6,6]$"] [17592186582398 "Find the limit at $x=0$ of the piecewise function $f(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}+1 & \\text{ for }\\,x<0 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ for }\\,x=0 \\\\ {e}^{-x}+1 & \\text{ for }\\,x>0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186582403 "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x\\sqrt{y},\\, & \\text{if}\\,0<x<1,\\,0<y<3, \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy),\\, & \\text{if}\\,0<x<1,\\,3<y<5.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n{∬}_{(0,1)\\times (0,5)}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=\n$$"] [17592186582413 "Evaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{y},\\, & \\text{if}\\,-1<x<2,\\,1<y<e, \\\\ xy,\\, & \\text{if}\\,2<x<4,\\,1<y<e,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand  $R$ is the domain of $f.$"] [17592186582416 "Find the left-sided and the right-sided limits at $x=4$ for the function\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2 & \\text{ for }\\,x<4 \\\\ {e}^{x-4}+1 & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}g(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,$\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}g(x)=\n$$\n{{field-2}}"] [17592186583023 "Consider the following function:\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2}+1, & x\\ne -1 \\\\ 0, & x=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \nWhich of the following statements are true regarding $g(x)?$\n\nI. $g(x)$ is defined at $x=-1$\n\nII. $\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)$ exists\n\nIII. $\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)=g(-1)$\n\nIV. $g(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-1$"] [17592186583028 "Consider the following function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}36-{x}^{3}, & x<3 \\\\ 9, & x=3 \\\\ {x}^{2}, & x>3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding $f(x)?$\n\nI. $f(x)$ is defined at $x=3$\n\nII. $\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)$\n\nIII. $\\lim_{x\\to 3}f(x)=f(3)$\n\nIV. $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=3$"] [17592186585036 "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}k{e}^{10x},\\, & x<0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186585038 "$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{2x},\\, & x\\le 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\le 0$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nIII. $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function"] [17592186585060 "$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+2},\\, & x\\le 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\le 0$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nIII. $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function"] [17592186585082 "$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x},\\, & x\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\le 1$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nIII. $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function"] [17592186585083 "Compute $P(X\\le -5)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}},\\, & x\\le -3, \\\\ 0,\\, & x>-3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186585109 "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{(4-x)^{3}},\\, & x\\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186586548 "Given that $X$ is a continuous random variable with the following probability density function, compute $\\text{E}[X].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}, & 0<x<1 \\\\ \\frac{3{x}^{2}}{14}, & 1\\le x<2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186586566 "A random variable $X$ has the probability distribution given below.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}, & x=1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}, & x=2 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}, & x=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$"] [17592186586568 "Given that $X$ is a continuous random variable with the following probability density function, compute $\\text{E}[X].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{ex}, & \\frac{1}{e}<x<1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}, & 1\\le x<e\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186586606 "Given that $X$ is a continuous random variable with the following probability density function, compute $\\text{E}[X].$ Give your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}, & 1<x<2 \\\\ \\frac{2}{9}, & 2\\le x<3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186586613 "A random variable $X$ has the probability distribution given below.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}, & x=1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}, & x=2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}, & x=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{3}].$"] [17592186587292 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{144}, & 0\\le x\\le 12 \\\\ 1, & x>12,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nfind $P(4<X\\le 6).$"] [17592186587329 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{e}^{-|2x/3|}}{3},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}h(x),\\, & x<0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{{e}^{-2x/3}}{2},\\, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x).$"] [17592186589115 "Find $\\text{E}[{e}^{-3X}],$ given that the continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x+1}, & x\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186589138 "Find $\\text{E}[\\frac{1}{X}],$ given that the continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x{e}^{-2x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186589978 "Find $\\text{E}[Y+1],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\, & 0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186589980 "Find $\\text{E}[X+Y],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186589982 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}cy,\\, & 0\\le y\\le \\frac{4}{3},\\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le 2-y, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$"] [17592186589986 "Find $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}+2Y],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186589988 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}c{x}^{2}{y}^{2},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$"] [17592186589994 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{{y}^{2}},\\, & 1\\le x\\le {y}^{3}\\le 8, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$"] [17592186589997 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4xy,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind $P(Y<2X-2{X}^{2}).$"] [17592186590018 "Let $D=[1,2]\\times [0,2].$ Which of the following functions are valid joint probability density functions?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle f(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2-xy,\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle g(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+y}{8},\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle h(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+2y}{8},\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186590025 "Find $\\text{E}[\\frac{1}{X}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{5},\\, & 2-y\\le x\\le \\frac{y+4}{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 2; \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186590048 "$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}, & \\,x=1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}, & \\,x=3 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind $\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=4]$ given that the conditional probability mass function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,4)$ is shown above."] [17592186590069 "Find $\\text{E}[\\frac{XY}{3}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3-3x; \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186590072 "$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,4)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{6}, & \\,y=2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}, & \\,y=4 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=4]$ given that the conditional probability mass function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,4)$ is shown above."] [17592186591633 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-(\\frac{x}{3}+3y)}, & x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the marginal PDF ${f}_{Y}(y).$"] [17592186591641 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}\\,{x}^{3}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{y}{2}}, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind $P(Y\\le 1).$"] [17592186591656 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe marginal CDF ${F}_{Y}(y)$ of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{Y}(y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & y<0 \\\\ g(y), & 0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 1 & y>1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhat is the function $g(y)?$"] [17592186591663 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x, & 0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind $P(-3<Y<1).$"] [17592186591671 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, & 0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the marginal PDF ${f}_{X}(x).$"] [17592186591672 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-(x+y)}, & x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe marginal CDF ${F}_{X}(x)$ of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ g(x), & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhat is the function $g(x)?$"] [17592186591687 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}), & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe marginal CDF ${F}_{Y}(y)$ of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{Y}(y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & y<0 \\\\ g(y), & 0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 1 & y>1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhat is the function $g(y)?$"] [17592186591701 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{16}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3}), & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind $P(Y\\ge 1).$"] [17592186591702 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}-1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & 0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the marginal PDF ${f}_{Y}(y).$"] [17592186591710 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-(x+y)}, & x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the marginal PDF ${f}_{X}(x).$"] [17592186591713 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10}{3}x{y}^{4}, & 1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind $P(Y<\\frac{4}{5}).$"] [17592186591721 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}, & x\\ge 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe marginal CDF ${F}_{X}(x)$ of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ g(x), & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhat is the function $g(x)?$"] [17592186593256 "The continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x)$ stated below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=e-1,$ compute $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x},\\, & 1\\le x\\le e, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186593274 "The continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x)$ stated below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{11}{12},$ compute $\\text{Var}[X].$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1-{x}^{2}, & 0<x<1, \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}, & 1\\le x<3, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186594147 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{3}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nFind $P(X<\\frac{2}{3}\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{1}{2}).$"]}
